<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="page2-inspection-examples" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Two Harder Examples: Water Pollution and Arguing over Marbles</title>

  <p>
    Both games on this page are still solved by inspection, but each one breaks
    something we could rely on before. Water Pollution has three players, so
    there is no payoff matrix to draw. Arguing over Marbles gives each player
    infinitely many strategies, so we cannot check them one at a time.
  </p>

  <assemblage xml:id="read-3-3">
    <title>Section 3.3, Water Pollution 1</title>
    <p>
      <alert>Read Section 3.3.</alert>
    </p>
    <p>
      This one you can read straight through. The reasoning is the same
      inspection we have been doing, just organized by how many firms purify
      rather than by cells of a matrix. Pay attention to that organization,
      since it is how we handle any game with more than two players.
    </p>
  </assemblage>

  <exercise xml:id="check-water-pollution">
    <statement>
      <p>
        The profiles where two firms purify and one pollutes are Nash
        equilibria. In those profiles the polluter gets <m>0</m> while each
        purifier gets <m>-1</m>. Why can a purifier not improve its own payoff
        by switching to polluting?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <choices randomize="yes">
      <choice correct="yes">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Because two firms would then be polluting, which triggers the
            treatment cost for everybody, and the switcher's payoff would drop
            from <m>-1</m> to <m>-3</m>.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Correct, and this is the free rider problem in miniature. The
            purifiers can see that the polluter is doing better than they are,
            and neither of them can do anything about it alone. Notice also
            that the profile where all three firms pollute is a Nash
            equilibrium too, so this game has four of them.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Because once a profile is a Nash equilibrium, the definition does
            not allow a player to change strategy.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            The definition never forbids anything. It says that if a player
            were to change strategy on their own, they would not end up better
            off. Working out what would happen after such a change is exactly
            how we test a profile.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Because purifying is a dominant strategy for every firm.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            It is not. If the other two firms both purify, a firm does better
            by polluting, since it saves the cost of purifying and the lake
            stays clean. That is precisely why the profile where all three
            purify fails to be an equilibrium.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
    </choices>
  </exercise>

  <p>
    Now for the harder one. Arguing over Marbles is the first game in the
    course where a player has infinitely many strategies to choose from, and
    it is worth slowing down for. We are going to read the setup and then work
    through the analysis together rather than reading it.
  </p>

  <assemblage xml:id="read-3-4-setup">
    <title>Section 3.4, Arguing over Marbles: the setup only</title>
    <p>
      <alert>Read the beginning of Section 3.4, stopping after the list of
      payoffs.</alert>
    </p>
    <p>
      That is the paragraph describing the two children and the three bulleted
      rules for the payoffs. Stop when the book begins inspecting strategy
      profiles. We will do that part below, and then you will read the book's
      version afterwards to compare.
    </p>
  </assemblage>

  <p>
    The payoff rules are easier to feel than to read. In the interactive
    below, move either child's number and watch which rule takes over.
  </p>

  <figure xml:id="fig-marbles-explorer">
    <caption>
      How the payoffs work. The argument ends when the first child gives up,
      so it lasts as long as the smaller of the two numbers.
    </caption>
    <interactive xml:id="interactive-marbles-explorer"
      iframe="ch3-graph/marbles-payoff-explorer.html" width="100%" aspect="7:6">
      <static>
        <p>
          A number line from 0 to 1 carrying both children's numbers, with a
          slider for each. A marker shows where the argument ends, at whichever
          number is smaller, and a sentence reports who gets the marbles. Both
          payoffs are displayed with the current numbers substituted in, and a
          table of the three payoff rules highlights whichever rule is in force.
          Moving one child's number past the other switches the highlighted
          rule and reverses who wins the marbles.
        </p>
      </static>
    </interactive>
  </figure>

  <p>
    With the payoffs in hand we can hunt for Nash equilibria. There are
    infinitely many strategy profiles, so checking them one at a time is
    hopeless. Instead we split them into regions and handle a whole region at
    once. The book uses three: profiles with <m>s \lt t</m>, profiles with
    <m>s = t</m>, and profiles with <m>s \gt t</m>. Every profile lands in
    exactly one of them.
  </p>

  <p>
    Start with <m>s \lt t</m>. That region splits further into three cases,
    and in each one your job is the same: decide whether either child can do
    better by changing her own number.
  </p>

  <figure xml:id="fig-marbles-s-lt-t">
    <caption>
      Inspecting the profiles with <m>s \lt t</m>.
    </caption>
    <interactive xml:id="interactive-marbles-s-lt-t"
      iframe="ch3-graph/marbles-s-less-than-t.html" width="100%" aspect="1:1">
      <static>
        <p>
          A three step walkthrough of the region where Player 1's number is
          smaller than Player 2's. Each step shows a number line with the two
          numbers positioned for that case, a shaded band marking which choices
          keep a profile inside the case, and the payoffs written symbolically.
          A multiple choice question asks which child, if any, can improve. The
          first two cases fail the definition, and the third, the single profile
          with <m>s = 0</m> and <m>t = 1</m>, is a Nash equilibrium. It is not a
          strict one, since Player 2 has other numbers that pay her exactly as
          well.
        </p>
      </static>
    </interactive>
  </figure>

  <p>
    That settles two of the three regions, because the game is symmetric:
    swapping the two children turns any profile with <m>s \lt t</m> into one
    with <m>s \gt t</m>, so the same work applies with the labels exchanged.
    All that is left is the profiles where the two
    children pick the same number.
  </p>

  <figure xml:id="fig-marbles-s-eq-t">
    <caption>
      Inspecting the profiles where both children pick the same number, so
      that <m>s = t</m>.
    </caption>
    <interactive xml:id="interactive-marbles-s-eq-t"
      iframe="ch3-graph/marbles-s-equals-t.html" width="100%" aspect="1:1">
      <static>
        <p>
          A two step walkthrough of the profiles where both children choose the
          same number and split the marbles. When that shared number is below 1,
          either child can do better by raising her number above the other's.
          When it is exactly 1, raising is impossible, but either child can do
          better by dropping to 0 and giving up at once. Neither case produces a
          Nash equilibrium, so the only equilibria of the whole game are
          <m>(0,1)</m> and <m>(1,0)</m>.
        </p>
      </static>
    </interactive>
  </figure>

  <p>
    It is worth naming the shape of what we just did, because it comes back
    whenever a strategy set is too big to check profile by profile:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <p>
        Split every strategy profile into a few regions, so that each profile
        lands in exactly one region.
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p>
        In each region, either name a player and a specific strategy she could
        switch to that pays her more, or show that no such switch exists.
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p>
        Use symmetry to skip any region that is a mirror image of one you have
        already done.
      </p>
    </li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    One detail from the second bullet is easy to miss and worth stating on its
    own. When you name the switch, the new strategy does not have to stay
    inside the region you are working in. A region tells us which profiles are
    being tested, not which strategies a player is allowed to pick. In the
    case with <m>s = 0</m> above, the only move that helps Player 1 takes her
    out of <m>s \lt t</m> entirely.
  </p>

  <assemblage xml:id="read-3-4-rest">
    <title>The Rest of Section 3.4</title>
    <p>
      <alert>Read the rest of Section 3.4.</alert>
    </p>
    <p>
      You have already done this reasoning, so move through the case analysis
      quickly and use it to check your own work against the book's. Do read the
      closing paragraphs properly: the book compares this game to Chicken and
      mentions that it is a version of the War of Attrition.
    </p>
  </assemblage>

  <assemblage xml:id="read-3-5">
    <title>Section 3.5, Tobacco Market (optional)</title>
    <p>
      <alert>Section 3.5 is optional. Read it if you are interested, or skip
      it.</alert>
    </p>
    <p>
      It is another inspection problem, this time with three players and
      continuous strategies, and it uses calculus to locate the candidate
      equilibrium. Nothing on this page depends on it, and we will meet the
      same technique in a cleaner form in Section 3.11 when we get to Cournot's
      duopoly. If you do read it, the piecewise payoff function caused by the
      price support is the same kind of thing we saw in Stackelberg's second
      model back in Section 1.10.
    </p>
  </assemblage>

  <interactive xml:id="question-form-3-2" iframe="question-form.html?section=3.2" width="100%" aspect="2:1"/>
</section>
